# Target C — all-regime wrapping / two-half-disk dichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only Type-II structural theorem; theorem authority unchanged.

This note removes the historical `x_B=o(D)` hypothesis from the geometric two-half-disk
alternative.  It uses only the current canonical Type-II attachment-cell tiling stack:
Sections 80, 83, and the cap-support geometry of Section 88.

The conclusion is qualitative and all-regime:

> Along every Type-II subsequence, after a further subsequence, either one positive-area
> harmonic-cell limit contains the disk center in its interior, or the harmonic cells
> split at leading area scale into exactly two complementary half-disk cap groups.  In
> the second alternative each group is defined by an actual harmonic-cell sequence of
> diameter `(1-o(1))D`.

No common diameter cap, small Section-101 scalar slack, outer-detour hypothesis, or
`x_B=o(D)` assumption is used.

---

## 1. Canonical Type-II inputs

Translate by the Type-II centers and scale by `D`.  Canonical Sections 80 and 83 give

\[
F_D/D\longrightarrow B_{1/2},
\tag{1.1}
\]

pairwise interior-disjoint attachment cells

\[
P_{r,D}=\operatorname{conv}(A_{r,D})\subset F_D,
\tag{1.2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r \operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})
=\left({\pi\over4}-o(1)\right)D^2.
}
\tag{1.3}
\]

They also give the square-root area bound

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r\sqrt{\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})}=O(D).
}
\tag{1.4}
\]

Finally the strict-rhombus localization in the same branch gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_r\left(
\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})-A_{4,r,D}
\right)=o(D^2),
}
\tag{1.5}
\]

where `A_{4,r,D}` is the strict-rhombus area assigned to cell `r`; every summand is
nonnegative.

For every fixed `eta>0`, (1.4) implies that cells of area `<eta D^2` carry total area
`O(sqrt(eta))D^2`.  Hence only finitely many cells above the cutoff matter at leading
area scale.  Passing to a subsequence, every retained cell converges to a positive-area
convex set

\[
Q_i=\operatorname{conv}(S_i)\subset B_{1/2},
\tag{1.6}
\]

with compact support `S_i` on the radius-`1/2` circle, and the `Q_i` retain pairwise
disjoint interiors.

---

## 2. Center-wrapping versus closed-cap type

Call a positive-area limit `Q=conv(S)` **center-wrapping** if

\[
0\in\operatorname{int}Q.
\tag{2.1}
\]

If (2.1) fails, separation through the origin gives a unit vector `n` with

\[
n\cdot x\ge0\qquad(x\in Q),
\]

so `S` lies in a closed semicircle.  Let `J(S)` be its minimal closed supporting circle
arc and write

\[
0<\alpha(S):=|J(S)|\le\pi.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Compactness makes both endpoints of the minimal arc members of `S`.  Its chord is
therefore a supporting edge of `Q`, and `Q` lies on the circular-cap side of that
chord.

Thus every positive-area limit is in exactly one of the two classes needed here:
center-wrapping, or closed-cap type with support span at most `pi`.  The latter includes
possible diameter-one limits whose support uses an antipodal pair; this is why the
closed-semicircle formulation is used instead of the open-semicircle wording of the
`lambda<1` part of Section 88.

---

## 3. Closed-cap support arcs remain laminar at the endpoint `alpha=pi`

### Lemma 3.1

For pairwise interior-disjoint positive-area closed-cap limits `Q_i`, their minimal
support arcs `J_i` are laminar: two relative interiors are disjoint or one arc is
contained in the other.

### Proof

Suppose two arcs overlap in their relative interiors and neither contains the other.
For arcs of length at most `pi`, their four extreme endpoints alternate around the
circle.  Hence the two base chords cross properly.  Each chord is a relative-interior
piece of a supporting line of its positive-area convex set, and each set lies on the
circular-cap side of its chord.  At a proper crossing, those two cap-side half-planes
have a common open sector.  Positive area on the supported side puts interior points of
both convex sets in that sector arbitrarily near the crossing, contradicting pairwise
interior disjointness.

The endpoint case `alpha=pi` causes no exception.  Its base chord is a diameter.  A
partially overlapping nonnested arc still has alternating endpoints and a proper chord
crossing.  Two complementary semicircles have disjoint arc interiors and are allowed.
If two distinct cells had the **same** minimal semicircle, they would have the same
diameter as a supporting edge and would lie on the same side of its line.  At every
relative-interior point of that diameter, each positive-area convex cell contains an
open inward wedge; the two wedges overlap.  Hence equal semicircle supports are also
impossible for distinct interior-disjoint positive-area cells. `square`

Retain the inclusion-maximal support arcs.  Lemma 3.1 plus the equal-arc exclusion makes
their relative interiors pairwise disjoint.  If their angular lengths are

\[
\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_s\in(0,\pi],
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i\alpha_i\le2\pi.
}
\tag{3.1}
\]

Every retained cell nested below one maximal arc lies in its radius-`1/2` circular cap.
Hence the whole retained group below that arc has total area at most

\[
G(\alpha_i):={\alpha_i-\sin\alpha_i\over8}.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Cells discarded by the area cutoff are not used to define these maximal retained arcs;
their **total area**, rather than their support spans, is paid by the `O(sqrt(eta))`
tail from (1.4).  Thus a very thin discarded cell with a long support arc cannot spoil
the capacity estimate for the retained family.

---

## 4. Disk-area saturation forces two semicircle groups

Assume now that **no** positive-area harmonic-cell limit is center-wrapping.  Apply the
preceding construction at a fixed cutoff `eta`.  Equations (1.3)--(1.4) give

\[
{\pi\over4}-O(\sqrt\eta)
\le
\sum_i G(\alpha_i)
\le
{1\over8}\sum_i\alpha_i
\le {\pi\over4}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Therefore

\[
2\pi-\sum_i\alpha_i=O(\sqrt\eta)
\tag{4.2}
\]

and, because

\[
\sum_iG(\alpha_i)
={1\over8}\sum_i\alpha_i
-{1\over8}\sum_i\sin\alpha_i,
\]

also

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i\sin\alpha_i=O(\sqrt\eta).
}
\tag{4.3}
\]

This already classifies the equality profile.  To make the cutoff passage explicit,
fix `epsilon in (0,pi/3)`.

- Arcs with `epsilon<=alpha_i<=pi-epsilon` contribute at least `sin epsilon` each to
  (4.3), so their number tends to zero as `eta->0`.
- Arcs with `alpha_i<epsilon` have
  \[
  G(\alpha_i)
  ={\alpha_i-\sin\alpha_i\over8}
  \le{\alpha_i^3\over48}
  \le{\epsilon^2\alpha_i\over48}.
  \]
  By (3.1), their total cap area is at most `pi epsilon^2/24`.
- At most two maximal arcs can have length above `pi-epsilon`, because three such arcs
  would have total length greater than `2pi`.

Let first `eta->0` and then `epsilon->0`.  Since the retained cell area tends to
`pi/4` while all small/intermediate maximal arcs carry vanishing cap area, exactly two
maximal retained arcs survive.  Their lengths approach `pi`, and their relative
interiors are disjoint.  Thus their limiting arcs are the two complementary
semicircles cut by one diameter of `B_{1/2}`.

The cutoff and source limits are taken by a standard finite diagonal.  Choose
`eta_j downarrow0`.  For each `j`, choose the source index far enough along the original
Type-II sequence that the area tail, Hausdorff cell limits, and inequalities
(4.1)--(4.3) hold with error `o_j(1)`.  Let `J_{1,j},J_{2,j}` be the two long maximal
retained arcs.  Compactness of the circle and a further subsequence give

\[
\boxed{
|J_{1,j}|\to\pi,
\qquad
|J_{2,j}|\to\pi,
}
\tag{4.4}
\]

with complementary limiting semicircles.  The corresponding finite-source cells and
groups are the sequences used below; no fixed positive cutoff is asked to produce an
exact semicircle.

Because each long group has cap capacity at most `pi/8+o(1)` and the two long groups
together carry `pi/4-o(1)` retained area, each separately saturates.  Restoring the
vanishing discarded tail gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}
\operatorname{area}(P_{r,D})
=\left({\pi\over8}-o(1)\right)D^2,
\qquad i=1,2.
}
\tag{4.5}

Every other retained maximal support group, together with the discarded cutoff tail,
has total area `o(D^2)` along the diagonal sequence.

This proves the leading-area two-half-disk decomposition without any outer-walk
smallness assumption.

---

## 5. Each half-disk group has a diameter-scale defining harmonic cell

For each `i=1,2`, the long maximal retained arc `J_{i,j}` belongs to an actual retained
harmonic cell; denote the corresponding diagonal finite-source sequence by
`P_{i,D}^*`.  The two extreme attachment supports converge to the endpoints of
`J_{i,j}`.  Their Euclidean chord length is

\[
D\sin(|J_{i,j}|/2)+o(D)=(1-o(1))D.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Both endpoints are attachments of `P_{i,D}^*`, hence

\[
\boxed{
{\operatorname{diam}(P_{i,D}^*)\over D}\to1,
\qquad i=1,2.
}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Canonical harmonic trapping then gives the same diameter scale for the component graph:
if `delta_{i,D}^*` is its contact-graph diameter,

\[
\operatorname{diam}(P_{i,D}^*)\le\delta_{i,D}^*+2,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{\delta_{i,D}^*\over D}\to1.
}
\tag{5.3}
\]

The two defining cells need not themselves carry all of the half-disk area: nested
components may share a dominant group.  Only their diameter-scale support span is
asserted.

---

## 6. Each group also carries half of the strict-rhombus area

Equation (1.5) has nonnegative summands.  Therefore the total local rhombus-area deficit
inside either dominant group is at most the global `o(D^2)` deficit.  Combining with
(4.5),

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{r\in\mathcal G_i}A_{4,r,D}
=\left({\pi\over8}-o(1)\right)D^2,
\qquad i=1,2.
}
\tag{6.1}
\]

Thus the no-wrapping alternative is not merely a convex-cell partition: both half-disk
groups contain quadratic strict-rhombus area and the full local train structure that
comes with it.

---

## 7. The all-regime dichotomy

Combining the preceding steps gives the structural theorem.

### Theorem 7.1 — wrapping or two half-disks

Along every Type-II survivor subsequence, after a further subsequence, at least one of
the following holds.

1. **Center-wrapping alternative.**  A positive-area harmonic-cell limit `Q` satisfies
   `0 in int(Q)`.

2. **Two-half-disk alternative.**  Along a finite diagonal there are two complementary
   dominant cap groups `G_1,G_2` satisfying (4.5) and (6.1), and each has a defining
   harmonic-cell sequence satisfying (5.2)--(5.3).  All remaining cell area is
   `o(D^2)` outside those two groups.

The alternatives are exhaustive.  The second alternative has no `x_B=o(D)`, common
cell-diameter cap, Section-101 endpoint, or cap-minimality hypothesis.

A center-wrapping limit of normalized diameter `<1` falls under the existing canonical
wrapping-support machinery.  A center-wrapping limit of diameter `1` is already a
near-global harmonic carrier and should be treated together with the near-diameter
branch rather than forced into the cap classification.

---

## 8. Frontier effect and nonclaims

The theorem converts arbitrary Type-II harmonic-cell geometry into two structural
branches:

```text
positive-area center-wrapping carrier
              OR
two complementary rhombic half-disk groups,
with one diameter-scale defining component on each side.
```

It does **not** yet close either branch.  In particular:

- the two diameter-scale defining components can be nested among additional cells;
- their near-geodesic contact routes need not be exact straight chains;
- no same-cardinality strict sub-`D` source is constructed;
- no exact unit triangle is inferred from the limiting half-disk equality;
- contact-isolated source points remain part of the full source and are untouched.

The next closing target in the second alternative is now specific: couple the two
quadratic rhombic train systems across their common limiting diameter, or show that the
two diameter-scale harmonic components force incompatible source-level diameter/contact
loads.

**Disposition:** new proof-only all-regime structural reduction.  C-II remains open.
